The rain was a persistent, grey drizzle, the kind that soaked you through without you even noticing. Elias Thorne, postman of the small village of Oakhaven for the last thirty-two years, barely registered it. He’d seen worse. He’d delivered mail through blizzards that piled snow up to his knees, through scorching summer heat that melted the ink on envelopes, and through the howling winds that threatened to rip his satchel from his shoulder. He was a fixture in Oakhaven, as reliable as the sunrise and the changing seasons.

Elias wasn't a man of grand gestures or booming pronouncements. He was quiet, almost taciturn, with a face etched with the gentle lines of time and a perpetually damp tweed jacket. He didn't chat much, offering a curt nod and a brief "Morning" or "Afternoon" to the villagers. But within that quiet exterior resided a deep understanding of Oakhaven and its inhabitants. He knew Mrs. Gable always received a postcard from her daughter in Australia, every Tuesday. He knew Mr. Henderson anxiously awaited the gardening catalogues, and that young Lily Bell always held her breath hoping for a letter from her pen pal in France.

